# core - the two types every program is allowed to assume.
#
# `maybe T` is a value or nothing. `result T` is a value or a message. They
# live here rather than in `orbs/option` and `orbs/result` because the syntax
# reference lists them among the types, next to `text` and `table`, and a type
# you have to import first is not one of those.
#
#   define find(k: number) -> maybe number:
#       if k > 0 then some(k * 10) else none
#
#   define div(a: number, b: number) -> result number:
#       if b is 0 then error("div by zero") else ok(a // b)
#
#   choose div(84, 2):
#       ok(v)    then v
#       error(m) then print_line(m)
#
# `some` / `none` / `ok` / `error` are the spoken names; `Some` / `None` /
# `Ok` / `Err` are what the variants are declared as, and both read. The
# spoken ones are resolved LAST, so a variant or a function of your own by
# that name keeps its meaning - `file.ok(handle)` still asks whether a handle
# is open.
#
# The FUNCTIONS over these types are still `use option` and `use result`.
# Only the types are free, because only the types are what the surface calls
# core. The two orbs share the name `unwrap_or`, so making both visible
# everywhere would make that one name ambiguous in every program.
#
# The whole file is two declarations on purpose. Anything else added here is
# added to every program in the language.

public type Option<T>:
    Some(T)
    None

public type Result<T>:
    Ok(T)
    Err(text)
